8. Etiquette
---

These are rules that cannot be enforced at a programmatic level. As such, your job may be cancelled without notice if it does not comply with these rules. If such a job is preventing you from using the cluster, reach out to the person and try and sort it out between yourselves, if possible. If this does not work, let us know and we will take the appropriate action.

(!) It goes without saying but always be respectful when talking to another user

Rules:

* Resource utilisation, especially GPU utilisation - there is an expectation that you know what your code is doing and have tried smaller jobs while monitoring GPU utilisation before launching a big job on the cluster. If a job has requested resources that it is not using most of the time and this is preventing users from launching jobs, it may be cancelled

* CPU jobs - some CPU only jobs are acceptable, e.g. installing/building dependencies, benchmarking against GPU runs, monitoring GPU/memory usage or other small jobs. Any large CPU jobs that do actual analysis for a long period of time/request a large number of CPUs are not allowed

* Saturating the cluster - most of the time, you should not saturate the cluster and submit many jobs to all the nodes at once as this will prevent someone else from getting a node for a long amount of time. Instead, you should target a subset of the nodes. If the cluster is relatively empty, you can send a message on the "GPU Slurm" Teams channel that you are intending to use all the nodes and that anyone can contact you to cancel any of your jobs. Your job may still be cancelled if you do not respond to people asking you to cancel your job

* Fragmenting the cluster - your jobs may end up in a state where many 1 GPU jobs are running across multiple nodes as they may have been picked up by different machines at different points in time. This prevents jobs which are waiting to be able to use all of the nodes on a single machine. For example, gpu3 and gpu4 have 4 GPU cards each. If you have a 1 GPU job on gpu3 and another on gpu4, then a user who wants to run a 4 GPU job cannot use gpu3 or gpu4 as they only have 3 cards available. In this case, one of your jobs may be cancelled. To prevent this, you are advised to use the `--nodelist` flag to target a specific machine for jobs that use fewer GPU cards than the machine offers. For example, submitting both 1 GPU jobs with `sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 script.sh` so that only gpu4 is fragmented and gpu3 remains free to use for 4 GPU jobs
